[description]

[b]Bitbucket Cloud[/b] tracks Atlassian’s SaaS roadmap: integrated pipelines, native Jira links, and marketplace apps. Teams choose it when they already live in Atlassian ID and want low ops.

[b]Bitbucket Data Center[/b] targets enterprises needing clustering, SAML, and data residency controls. Upgrade cadence and sizing guides differ from Cloud—capacity planning for Git storage and search indices matters.

[b]Server end-of-life:[/b] many orgs migrated Server installs to Cloud or Data Center; if you inherit old docs, verify which edition they reference—API paths and features diverge.

[b]Repositories and projects:[/b] Bitbucket groups repositories into [i]projects[/i] with permissions and hooks scoped per project or repo. Mirror GitHub’s org/repo mental model but read Atlassian’s permission names literally.

[b]Pipelines vs external CI:[/b] Bitbucket Pipelines is YAML in-repo; you can also wire Bamboo, Jenkins, or GitHub Actions elsewhere via webhooks—avoid double-triggering builds.

[b]Takeaway:[/b] edition choice is a compliance and billing decision; Git operations remain standard—focus migration risk on hooks, apps, and Jira field mappings.
